export const advancedReactPatterns = "## Introduction\n\nMost React apps don't collapse under load. They collapse under their own weight.\n\nA component that started as 40 clean lines becomes 400 lines of tangled props, conditional rendering, and effects nobody wants to touch. The app still works — but every new feature takes a little longer than the last, and one day a \"quick change\" takes three days.\n\nScalability on the frontend isn't only about performance. It's about how well your codebase **absorbs change**. The patterns below are the ones that consistently keep large React UIs flexible, testable, and genuinely pleasant to work in. We'll go from the everyday (composition) to the modern (Server Components).\n\n<div class=\"callout c-rec\"><span class=\"ic\">🎯</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Recruiter insight</b><p>In interviews, senior React roles rarely ask \"what is useState.\" They ask how you'd <em>structure</em> a growing feature. Knowing these patterns by name — and their trade-offs — signals experience more than any single API does.</p></div></div>\n\n## The decision map\n\nBefore the patterns themselves, here's the mental model that ties them together. Almost every scaling decision comes down to one question: **where should this responsibility live?**\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A([New piece of state or logic]) --> B{Used by only one component?}\n    B -- Yes --> C[Keep it local: useState / useReducer]\n    B -- No --> D{Comes from the server?}\n    D -- Yes --> E[Server-state library: TanStack Query]\n    D -- No --> F{Shared by a few siblings?}\n    F -- Yes --> G[Lift to nearest parent or feature context]\n    F -- No --> H[App-wide store only if truly global]\n```\n\nKeep this picture in mind — most of the patterns below are just different answers to \"where should this live?\"\n\n## Composition over configuration\n\nThe fastest way to make a component unscalable is to keep adding props for every new case. You start with a friendly `<Button>`, and six months later it has `isLoading`, `iconLeft`, `iconRight`, `variant`, `fullWidth`, `tooltip`, and a `renderCustomContent` escape hatch.\n\nThis is **prop explosion** — a sign you're *configuring* when you should be *composing*. Composition lets consumers assemble behavior from smaller pieces instead of flipping flags.\n\n```tsx\n// Configuration-heavy — every new need is another prop\n<Card title=\"Revenue\" subtitle=\"Q4\" badge=\"New\" footerText=\"Updated today\" />\n\n// Composition — the consumer controls the layout\n<Card>\n  <Card.Header>\n    <Card.Title>Revenue</Card.Title>\n    <Badge>New</Badge>\n  </Card.Header>\n  <Card.Footer>Updated today</Card.Footer>\n</Card>\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-tip\"><span class=\"ic\">💡</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Pro tip</b><p>Keep one small escape hatch — a <code>className</code> or a <code>children</code> slot — so consumers can extend without you predicting every case. Composition scales because it doesn't try to.</p></div></div>\n\n## Compound components\n\nCompound components are the pattern behind that `Card.Header` syntax. A parent manages shared state, and its children read that state implicitly through **context** — so the pieces work together without the consumer wiring anything up.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createContext, useContext, useState } from \"react\";\n\nconst TabsContext = createContext<{\n  active: string;\n  setActive: (id: string) => void;\n} | null>(null);\n\nfunction Tabs({ defaultValue, children }: {\n  defaultValue: string; children: React.ReactNode;\n}) {\n  const [active, setActive] = useState(defaultValue);\n  return (\n    <TabsContext.Provider value={{ active, setActive }}>\n      {children}\n    </TabsContext.Provider>\n  );\n}\n\nTabs.List = TabList;\nTabs.Tab = Tab;\nTabs.Panel = TabPanel;\n```\n\nNow the consumer writes clean, declarative markup with zero state management — which is exactly how libraries like Radix UI and Shadcn scale to hundreds of use cases.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-int\"><span class=\"ic\">🔥</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Interview question</b><p>\"How would you build a Tabs component where the tabs and panels don't need to know about each other?\" → Compound components + context is the textbook answer. Mention <code>aria-selected</code> and you've shown you care about accessibility too.</p></div></div>\n\n## Custom hooks: split logic from view\n\nA component that both fetches data *and* renders it is doing two jobs. When either gets complex, the file becomes hard to read and impossible to reuse. Custom hooks extract the **behavior**, leaving the component with only the **view**.\n\n```tsx\n// Logic lives in a hook — reusable and testable in isolation\nfunction useProductSearch(query: string) {\n  const [results, setResults] = useState<Product[]>([]);\n  const [status, setStatus] = useState<\"idle\" | \"loading\" | \"error\">(\"idle\");\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (!query) return;\n    let cancelled = false;              // guard against race conditions\n    setStatus(\"loading\");\n    searchProducts(query)\n      .then((data) => { if (!cancelled) { setResults(data); setStatus(\"idle\"); } })\n      .catch(() => { if (!cancelled) setStatus(\"error\"); });\n    return () => { cancelled = true; }; // cleanup on re-run/unmount\n  }, [query]);\n\n  return { results, status };\n}\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-warn\"><span class=\"ic\">⚠️</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Common mistake</b><p>Fetching in <code>useEffect</code> without a <code>cancelled</code> flag. If the query changes fast, an older response can land after a newer one and overwrite it. The cleanup guard above prevents that stale-data bug.</p></div></div>\n\n## Colocate state, lift only when shared\n\nA classic scaling mistake is dumping everything in a global store \"just in case.\" Global state is powerful, but it couples unrelated parts of your app. The healthier default: keep state as close as possible to where it's used, and lift it only when two or more components truly need it.\n\n| State type | Example | Where it belongs |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Local UI** | Dropdown open, input value | `useState` in the component |\n| **Shared feature** | Active dashboard filters | Nearest parent / feature context |\n| **Server data** | Orders, users, products | TanStack Query (see next) |\n| **Truly global** | Theme, auth session | App-wide store — sparingly |\n\n## Treat server state as its own category\n\nHalf the state-management pain in React comes from treating data fetched from an API like local UI state. They're fundamentally different: server data goes stale, needs caching, refetching, deduplication, and background updates. Libraries like **TanStack Query** handle all of that for you.\n\n```tsx\nfunction useOrders() {\n  return useQuery({\n    queryKey: [\"orders\"],\n    queryFn: fetchOrders,\n    staleTime: 60_000,   // treat as fresh for 1 minute\n  });\n}\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-perf\"><span class=\"ic\">🚀</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Performance tip</b><p>Set a sensible <code>staleTime</code>. It's the single biggest lever for cutting redundant network calls — the cache serves data instantly and refetches quietly in the background.</p></div></div>\n\n## Model complex flows as state machines\n\nBooleans multiply badly. `isLoading`, `isError`, `isSuccess`, `isSubmitting` — combine a few and you get states that *should* be impossible but aren't (loading *and* error at once). For real flows — wizards, checkout, uploads — an explicit state machine is far more robust.\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> idle\n    idle --> uploading: select file\n    uploading --> success: done\n    uploading --> error: failed\n    error --> uploading: retry\n    success --> [*]\n```\n\n```typescript\ntype UploadState =\n  | { status: \"idle\" }\n  | { status: \"uploading\"; progress: number }\n  | { status: \"success\"; url: string }\n  | { status: \"error\"; message: string };\n```\n\nNow the type system guarantees you can't render a progress bar in the `error` state — that combination literally doesn't exist.\n\n## Structure by feature, not by file type\n\nHow you organize files quietly decides how the app scales. Grouping by type looks tidy at first — until changing one feature means jumping across three folders, with nothing telling you which files belong together.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-free\"><span class=\"ic\">💼</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Freelancer advice</b><p>Feature-based structure is a gift to your clients. When you hand off a project, a new developer can find everything for a feature in one folder — which makes your work look professional and reduces \"how does this work?\" follow-ups after delivery.</p></div></div>\n\n## Lean into Server Components\n\nWith React 19 and frameworks like Next.js, **React Server Components** change what \"scalable\" even means. Components that only read data and render markup can run on the server — shipping *zero* JavaScript to the browser for that part of the UI. You reserve client components (`\"use client\"`) for the parts that actually need interactivity.\n\n```tsx\n// Server Component — data fetched on the server, no JS shipped\nasync function ProductPage({ id }: { id: string }) {\n  const product = await getProduct(id);\n  return (\n    <article>\n      <h1>{product.name}</h1>\n      <p>{product.description}</p>\n      <AddToCartButton productId={id} />  {/* client component */}\n    </article>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-perf\"><span class=\"ic\">🚀</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Performance tip</b><p>As your app grows, its JS bundle no longer has to grow with it — static and data-driven parts stay on the server. This is the biggest scalability shift in React since hooks.</p></div></div>\n\n## Quick reference\n\nThe whole article on one screen — reach for the right pattern by the problem you're facing.\n\n| Pattern | Use it when… | Key tool |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Composition** | A component keeps sprouting props | `children`, slots |\n| **Compound components** | Related parts must work as a set | Context |\n| **Custom hooks** | Logic is tangled with rendering | Hooks |\n| **Colocate state** | You're reaching for a global store | `useState` |\n| **Server state** | Data comes from an API | TanStack Query |\n| **State machines** | Boolean flags are multiplying | Typed unions / XState |\n| **Feature structure** | Files for one feature are scattered | Folders by feature |\n| **Server Components** | Bundle size grows with the app | React 19 / Next.js |\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n<div class=\"takeaways\"><ul>\n<li>Scalability is about how well code <strong>absorbs change</strong>, not just raw performance.</li>\n<li>Compose from small pieces instead of adding props for every case.</li>\n<li>Keep state close; lift it only when genuinely shared; treat server data as its own thing.</li>\n<li>Model complex flows as typed states so impossible combinations can't happen.</li>\n<li>Organize by feature, and let the server render everything that doesn't need to be interactive.</li>\n<li>The one test for any component: <em>if you can't explain it in one sentence, it's doing too much.</em></li>\n</ul></div>\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Do I need all eight patterns in every project?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>No. Small apps thrive on just composition and colocated state. Reach for the others as specific pain appears — a growing prop list, tangled fetch logic, multiplying boolean flags. Patterns are answers to problems, not a checklist.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Is Redux dead now that we have Server Components and TanStack Query?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Not dead, but its role shrank. Most of what teams once used Redux for was actually server state — now better handled by TanStack Query. Redux (or Zustand) still fits truly global client state, but it's no longer the default reach.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>When should I use Context vs a state library?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Context is great for low-frequency, feature-scoped sharing (theme, current tab, auth). For high-frequency updates across many components, a dedicated store avoids unnecessary re-renders.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Do Server Components replace client components?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>No — they complement them. Think server-first: render everything static or data-driven on the server, and drop in small client \"islands\" wherever the user actually interacts.</p></div></details>\n";
